# Keep correlating docs in sync

When you change product behavior, public CLI/UI copy, install/setup flows, skills, or operator procedures in this repo, update the matching docs in the **same change** (same PR / same commit set). Do not leave docs describing the old behavior.

## What to update

Search for and revise every stale reference, typically under:

- `README.md`
- `docs/` (especially `getting-started.md` and any feature-specific doc)
- `CLAUDE.md` / `CONTRIBUTING.md` when bootstrap or contributor steps change
- Sample transcripts and demo strings that mirror user-visible output (e.g. `docs/install-transcript.txt`, quick-install demo text)
- Skill / onboarding docs that teach the changed flow

## How to decide scope

- User-facing behavior change → update the doc that operators read for that flow.
- Rename a flag, path, or command → grep the old spelling across `*.md` and fix hits.
- If no doc exists yet and the change introduces a new operator-facing path, add a short section to the closest existing doc (prefer `docs/getting-started.md` or the feature doc) rather than shipping silent behavior.

## Non-goals

- Do not invent large new doc sets for tiny internal refactors with no operator impact.
- Do not update untracked personal notes unless the user asks.
- Do not document expected reliability fixes that operators already assume — e.g. idempotent
  re-run after a cancelled/partial install, clean wipe/retry of leftover state, or other
  “it just recovers” behavior. Users do not need an implementation note for that; cover it
  with tests, not README / getting-started prose. Still update docs when the *operator
  steps*, flags, paths, or defaults change.
